{-
   The four ways a global can claim a compiler role wrongly - `compiler/intrinsic/supplied.def`.

   # resolver: ownership

   Each refusal is a statement about the *direction the fact travels*, which is the whole of what
   separates the two kinds of role. A written role is storage the entry sequence fills at run time,
   so it has to be `&` and its initializer is what a build where nothing filled it would hold. A
   supplied role is the other way: the compiler decides, so nothing may write it and its initializer
   has to be the zero of its type - a written number the compiler overwrites is a line of source that
   lies to whoever reads it.

   The type is checked coarsely and on purpose. What it catches is `@builtin(commandLineValues)` on a
   `Bool`, which would have a backend store an address into one byte of storage; what it deliberately
   does not do is restate the library's declarations here, which is the thing this whole directory
   removes.

   The roles are the ones the prelude leaves unfilled. `pageBytes` is `Native/Memory.yana`'s, and a
   second claim of it is refused before any of this is looked at - `Reject.Roles.yana` is where that
   is the point.
-}

-- Supplied, and something writes it.
@builtin(vectorBytes)
let &writable = 0 :: Size

-- Supplied, and the initializer claims to say something.
@builtin(wordBits)
let written = 32 :: Size

-- Written by the entry sequence, and declared immutable.
@builtin(commandLineEnvironment)
let notWritable = 0 :: %(%U8)

-- And a word role on something that is not a word - an `I64` is not one either, being
-- a width the language states rather than the one the machine picks.
@builtin(commandLineCount)
let &narrow = 0 :: I8

fn main() -> I32 = 0
